How APIs can be used to interact with foundation models
What is Amazon Bedrock?

Amazon Bedrock is an AWS service that provides access to foundation models through APIs.

Instead of building and maintaining the infrastructure needed to train and host large AI models, developers can use models available through Bedrock and integrate them into their applications.

Amazon Bedrock can be used to build applications that generate and process content using Generative AI models.

Foundation Models

A foundation model (FM) is a large, pre-trained AI model that can be adapted or used for many different tasks.

Foundation models can be used for tasks such as:

Text generation
Question answering
Summarization
Classification
Content generation
Code generation
Image generation
Semantic search
Other Generative AI applications

Amazon Bedrock provides access to foundation models from multiple providers.

The model selected depends on the requirements of the application, such as:

Type of task
Performance
Cost
Response quality
Context requirements
Supported capabilities
Model Inference

Inference is the process of using a trained model to generate an output from an input.

For example, a user can provide a prompt asking a model to explain a technical concept. The model processes the input and generates a response.

In Amazon Bedrock, applications can send requests to a foundation model and receive generated responses.

Inference can be performed through APIs and can be integrated into applications.

APIs

Amazon Bedrock provides APIs that applications can use to interact with foundation models.

A typical interaction involves:

The application creates a request.
The request contains the input or prompt.
Amazon Bedrock sends the request to the selected model.
The model processes the input.
The model generates a response.
The response is returned to the application.

This makes it possible to integrate Generative AI capabilities into applications without manually hosting the model.

Prompting

A prompt is the input provided to a Generative AI model.

For example:

Explain what Amazon S3 is to someone who has never used AWS.

The quality and structure of a prompt can influence the model's response.

Important prompting concepts include:

Clear instructions
Providing context
Defining the desired output
Giving examples when appropriate
Specifying constraints
Avoiding unnecessary ambiguity

Prompt engineering is therefore an important skill when working with Generative AI.

Tokenization

Language models process text using tokens.

A token can represent:

A complete word
Part of a word
A character
Punctuation
Other pieces of text

Text is converted into tokens before being processed by a language model.
Understanding tokens is important because models have limits related to the amount of information they can process in a request.
Token usage can also affect the cost of using Generative AI services.

Embeddings

An embedding is a numerical representation of information, such as text.
Instead of representing text only as words, an embedding represents the meaning or characteristics of the text as a vector.
Embeddings are useful for applications that need to compare the meaning of different pieces of information.

Common use cases include:

Semantic search
Similarity searches
Knowledge bases
Recommendation systems
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)

Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) combines information retrieval with Generative AI.

Instead of relying only on the information contained within a model, an application can retrieve relevant information from an external knowledge source and provide it to the model.
RAG can be useful when an application needs to generate responses based on specific or external information.
For example, a company could use RAG to allow an AI application to answer questions using internal company documents.

Fine-Tuning

Fine-tuning involves adapting a pre-trained model using additional data for a specific purpose or use case.
Fine-tuning can be useful when an organization needs model behavior that is better suited to a particular task or domain.
It is important to distinguish fine-tuning from prompting. Prompting changes the instructions provided to a model, while fine-tuning changes the model using additional training.

Amazon Bedrock Guardrails

Amazon Bedrock Guardrails help organizations apply controls to Generative AI applications.
Guardrails can be used to help control interactions between users and foundation models.

They can help address areas such as:

Harmful content
Undesired topics
Sensitive information
Unsafe inputs
Unsafe outputs
Responsible AI requirements

Guardrails are an important part of developing AI applications that need additional safety and content controls.

